<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Bali-Nyonga</h1><p class="st-meta">1835 CE – 1891 CE (approx.) · capital Bali</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Chamba warrior kingdom in the Cameroon Grassfields that became Germany's key ally in the region in the 1890s.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Bali-Nyonga descends from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chamba-state">Chamba</a>, mounted raiders who left the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benue-river">Benue</a> region in the early 19th century, perhaps fleeing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fulani-war">Fulani jihad</a>, and swept into the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon-grassfields">Grassfields</a>. After the death of their leader the raiders split, and one group, named after its leader Nyonga, settled near what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamenda">Bamenda</a> in the 1830s or later.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chamba-state">Chamba</a> conquerors adopted many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon-grassfields">Grassfields</a> institutions, including palace societies and the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fon-title">Fon</a>, while dominating local peoples by force. Under Fon Galega I, who ruled from about 1856 to 1901, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a> became the strongest power in the area. In 1889 Galega allied with the German explorer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eugen-zintgraff">Eugen Zintgraff</a>, and Bali forces fought with the Germans against Mankon in 1891.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The alliance brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a> into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kamerun">Kamerun</a> as a favoured German ally, supplied with guns and authority over its neighbours. After 1916 it passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/british-cameroons">British Cameroons</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fon-title">Fon</a> of Bali-Nyonga still reigns.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/bali-nyonga.jpg" alt="Bali-Nyonga" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Ricordisamoa · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Old_Flag_of_Bali.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1835 CE – 1891 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bali</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Galega I, Fonyonga II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Grassfields traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Mungaka</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/cameroon">Cameroon</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Bali Nyonga, Kingdom of Bali</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1863&amp;focus=bali-nyonga" aria-label="Bali-Nyonga on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4958 1958 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Bali-Nyonga lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5278 2214L5256 2278M5278 2214L5256 2278"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5256 2278h0M5256 2278h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5278 2214h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5278 2214h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Bali. <a href="/atlas?year=1863&amp;focus=bali-nyonga">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

